# Machine-wide additions to bash-allowlist-guard.sh's DEFAULT_ALLOWED.
# One bare binary name per line; '#' comments and blank lines are ignored.
# A project can add more in <repo>/.claude/bash-allowlist (additive).
#
# Admission rule for this file: the binary must be incapable of writing,
# deleting, or executing something else. That last clause is the important
# one — anything that takes another command as an argument (timeout, xargs,
# env, sudo, nice, watch, sh/bash/zsh -c) turns default-deny into
# default-allow, because the guard only checks the FIRST word of each
# command position. Those are deliberately absent and should stay absent.
#
# Chosen from measurement, not guesswork: these cleared 19 of the 27 blocks
# recorded across five sessions (head 6, echo 5, cd 4, tail 3, wc 1). The
# remaining 8 were pip (correctly denied — use uv), zsh (runs arbitrary
# scripts), printenv (dumps the whole environment including secrets, denied
# on purpose, see below), and five parser artifacts that no allowlist can
# fix: fd's `--exec ... \;` terminator, heredoc bodies, and `python -c`
# strings containing a nested double quote.

# --- read-only text and output shaping -----------------------------------
head
tail
wc
cat
cut
tr
comm
diff

# --- read-only path and file inspection ----------------------------------
basename
dirname
stat
file
ls

# --- read-only environment and scalars -----------------------------------
# printenv is deliberately absent: with no args it dumps the whole
# environment, including any secret sitting in an env var. Read a named,
# known-safe var with a dedicated tool instead of a blanket dump.
echo
date
seq

# --- shell builtin ---------------------------------------------------------
# Harmless alone: it changes the working directory and nothing else. What
# follows it is still parsed as its own command position, so
# `cd /x && rm -rf y` still blocks on rm.
cd
python3
